  "content": {
    "id": "p21-q18-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "18",
    "prompt": "Show that there is only one set of consecutive integers $a<b<c<d$ for which $a^3+b^3+c^3=d^3$.",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [],
    "answer": {
      "short": "$(a,b,c,d)=(3,4,5,6)$.\n\nWriting the integers as $n-1,n,n+1,n+2$ gives a monic cubic whose only integer root is $n=4$.",
      "worked": "Write $a=n-1$, $b=n$, $c=n+1$ and $d=n+2$, where $n\\in\\mathbb Z$.\n\n$$\\begin{align*}(n-1)^3+n^3+(n+1)^3&=(n+2)^3\\\\3n^3+6n&=n^3+6n^2+12n+8\\\\n^3-3n^2-3n-4&=0\\end{align*}$$\n\nLet $P(n)=n^3-3n^2-3n-4$. By the Integer Root Theorem, any integer root is among $\\pm1,\\pm2,\\pm4$.\n\n$$\\begin{align*}P(1)&=-9,&P(-1)&=-5\\\\P(2)&=-14,&P(-2)&=-18\\\\P(4)&=0,&P(-4)&=-104\\end{align*}$$\n\nThus $n=4$ is the only possible integer value, giving $(a,b,c,d)=(3,4,5,6)$. Checking,\n\n$$3^3+4^3+5^3=27+64+125=216=6^3.$$\n\nHence this is the unique set in increasing order.",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
    "answerSpaceMm": 90
  },
